ow or failed write for one trade blocked otherwise independent trades.\n\n## Root cause\n\nThe implementation used one module-local `pending` chain for every trade and appended work with only a fulfillment handler. A rejection therefore poisoned the chain. State was placed in `states` before awaiting `applyToStore`, violating the persistence/commit boundary. The reducer was also called without lifecycle, version, terminal-state, or payload validation, and mutable event data could cross the asynchronous and callback boundaries.\n\n## Fix\n\nThe projector now maintains a recoverable promise tail per trade ID. Each operation waits only for prior work on the same trade, while its stored tail consumes either fulfillment or rejection so subsequent work can proceed. Candidate state is validated and reduced, passed to persistence as a clone, and committed only after persistence succeeds.\n\nThe repair also enforces first-event, contiguous-version, stale/duplicate, and terminal-state rules; validates event shape and payload fields; and clones accepted inputs, persistence arguments, returned states, and snapshots.\n\n## Regression coverage\n\n`student-tests.mjs` adds five tests covering:\n\n- independent progress across trades and ordered work within one trade;\n- recovery and rollback after persistence rejection;\n- stale-event idempotency, version gaps, and cancellation terminality;\n- lifecycle, event-type, and payload validation, including partial amendments;\n- isolation from mutation of inputs, callback arguments, results, and snapshots.\n\nThe committed tests and student regressions pass together: 7 tests passed, 0 failed.\n\n## Remaining risk\n\nVerification uses the fixture's deterministic in-memory tests; it does not exercise a real persistence service or process crash between an external write and the in-memory commit. The callback remains responsible for durable-store idempotency if a caller retries after an ambiguous external failure.\n"}}],"api":"openai-completi